    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "On the North Slope of Alaska, Iñupiaq is the indigenous language that has been spoken for thousands of years. Due to many factors, the language is not the strong prominent language it once was. Young Iñupiaq people are not given the opportunity to learn their language but they are eager to learn and begin speaking. To help achieve the goal of making Iñupiaq spoken by all generations, the language blocks will be a tool to teach young people the basic grammatical rules of sentence construction. The blocks will provide students with instant feedback while they are beginning to learn how word combinations come together so they can hear out loud the parts of speech as they practice. The target audience for these blocks will be middle school students who already have a basic introduction to Iñupiaq with limited vocabulary. The problem that often occurs with these middle school children while learning the language is the lack of an opportunity to use and practice the spoken language. As a result learners are not learning how to properly construct grammatically correct sentences. With the blocks students will be able to rapidly test multiple sentence combinations until the grammatically correct sentence is discovered. The physical nature of the blocks will allow for learners to explore combinations of words quickly with no penalty, providing a safe environment for exploration that keep students engaged in the learning experience. Learners will be assessed through their understanding based on their ability to construct complete sentences once the blocks are removed. The blocks are only intended to get the students to understand the rules so they can begin using the language.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
